{"entries":[{"caption":"","key":"fig-ge-suite","order":{"number":1,"section":[8,4,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"Figure 7.1. Data quality score over 24 months under three testing regimes. Without tests, quality degrades monotonically toward the floor. Periodic tests produce a sawtooth: quality recovers after each test cycle but decays between them. Continuous testing maintains quality near its ceiling because failures are detected and corrected before significant degradation accumulates. The gap between periodic and continuous testing is largest when the drift rate is high relative to the test interval.","key":"fig-quality-drift","order":{"number":2,"section":[8,6,0,0,0,0,0]}}],"headings":["the-puzzle","the-four-dimensions-of-spatial-data-quality","tests-with-dbt","great-expectations-for-non-dbt-pipelines","data-lineage","simulating-data-quality-drift","governance-policies","exercises","build-this"],"options":{"chapters":true}}